Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is the “ability to consummate” concept subject to Material Adverse Effect (MAE) carveouts?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: “Material Adverse Effect” means any event, change, circumstance, condition, development, or effect (each, an “Effect”) that, individually or in the aggregate, has had or would reasonably be expected to have a material adverse effect on (i) the financial condition, business, or results of operations of the Company and its Subsidiaries, taken as a whole, or (ii) the ability of the Company to consummate the transactions contemplated by this Agreement; provided, however, that, for purposes of clause (i) only, none of the following shall be deemed to be or constitute a “Material Adverse Effect” or shall be taken into account when determining whether a “Material Adverse Effect” has occurred or may, would, or could occur: (a) changes in general economic or political conditions or conditions in the industries in which the Company operates, except to the extent that the Company is disproportionately adversely affected relative to other companies in the same industry; (b) changes in applicable laws or regulations or interpretations thereof; (c) the announcement of this Agreement or the transactions contemplated hereby; (d) any failure by the Company to meet internal or external projections, forecasts, or estimates of revenues or earnings (provided that the underlying causes of such failure may be considered unless otherwise excluded by this definition); (e) any action taken or omitted to be taken pursuant to the terms of this Agreement or with the written consent of the other party. (Pages 45-47)\nAnswer:
Choices:

