Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is the “ability to consummate” concept subject to Material Adverse Effect (MAE) carveouts?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: “Company Material Adverse Effect” means any change, event, occurrence, effect or circumstance (each, an “Effect”) that, individually or in aggregate with other Effects, (i) has had or would reasonably be expected to have a material adverse effect on the business, assets, liabilities, results of operations or financial condition of the Company and its Subsidiaries, taken as a whole, or (ii) would, or would reasonably be expected to, prevent, materially delay or materially impair the ability of the Company to consummate the transactions contemplated by this Agreement; provided, however, that in the case of clause (i) only, none of the following shall be deemed to constitute a Company Material Adverse Effect: (a) changes in general economic conditions or conditions in the financial markets, credit markets or capital markets in any country or region; (b) changes in conditions in the industries in which the Company operates; (c) changes in applicable laws, regulations or accounting principles; (d) any natural disasters or acts of terrorism, sabotage or war; (e) the announcement or pendency of this Agreement, including any impact on relationships with employees, customers or suppliers; (f) any decline in the market price or trading volume of the Company’s securities, in and of itself; (g) any failure by the Company to meet any internal or external projections or forecasts; (h) any action taken or omitted to be taken by the Company with the express written approval of the Parent; provided further, that with respect to clauses (a) through (d), such Effects may be taken into account in determining a Company Material Adverse Effect if they have a materially disproportionate impact on the Company relative to other entities in the same industry. (Pages 12-13)\nAnswer:
Choices:

