Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is the “ability to consummate” concept subject to Material Adverse Effect (MAE) carveouts?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: “Material Adverse Effect” means any effect, change, event, circumstance, condition, occurrence or development that, individually or in the aggregate, has had or would reasonably be expected to have a material adverse effect on (i) the business, properties, assets, liabilities, results of operations or financial condition of such party and its Subsidiaries, taken as a whole, or (ii) the ability of such party to consummate the transactions contemplated hereby; provided, however, that, with respect to clause (i) only, Material Adverse Effect shall not include the impact of (A) changes in GAAP or regulatory accounting requirements, (B) changes in laws, rules or regulations of general applicability, (C) changes in global, national or regional political conditions or economic or market conditions affecting the industry generally, (D) acts of war, terrorism, natural disasters, or pandemics, except to the extent that such events have a disproportionate effect on the party compared to other industry participants, (E) changes in stock price or trading volume, or (F) failure to meet financial projections, except that the underlying causes of such failure may be taken into account.\nAnswer:
